hi this is dr. Gregory Sadler I'm a  professor of philosophy and the  president and founder of an educational  consulting company called reason IO  where we put philosophy into practice  I've studied and taught philosophy for  over 20 years and I find that many  people run into difficulties reading  classic philosophical texts sometimes  it's the way things are said or how the  text is structured but the concepts  themselves are not always that  complicated and that's where I come in  to help students and lifelong learners  I've been producing longer lecture  videos and posting them to youtube many  viewers say they find them useful what  you're currently watching is part of a  new series of shorter videos each of  them focused on one core concept from an  important philosophical text I hope you  find it useful as well one of the most  important concepts in Rainer Maria Rilke  is letters to a young poet is what we  translate in English typically as  solitude in German it's Ines um kite and  another translation for it that you'll  see sometimes used in his poetry is  loneliness because I need some means  being on one's own enzym kite is the  state of being on one's own and for  Rilke this is not just something that  you could say predicate of a person who  happens accidentally to be on his or her  own instead it's something that belongs  to a person that in some respect makes  them the person who they are what they  do with their solitude and he considers  it to be an essential part of the human  condition but it's not necessarily a  feature of that condition that everybody  manages well or understands or reacts to  in the the right ways and every  individual person every human being is  not only capable of solitude of having  some sort of interior aura t an inner  life however you want to put it  something that is their own that makes  them they're the individual who they are  apart from although it could still be  engaged with other individuals  Rilke goes beyond that he actually  suggests that it is absolutely central  to the human condition so in letter 8 he  says to speak of solitude again it  becomes clearer and clearer that  fundamentally this is nothing that one  can choose or refrain from we are  solitary this is what we are we are  lightens up we are on our own to some  degree we can delude ourselves about  this that's a possibility that's a  choice and act as if this were not true  that is all but how much better it is to  recognize that we are alone yes even to  begin from this realization he says and  if we do begin from this realization  it's going to help us over time not  immediately he talks about being dizzy  when thinking about this but it will  help us over time to develop into a  state where we can make use of that that  solitude in an earlier letter number 5  when he is writing to this this young  man he suggests to us that solitude is  is not something that we can actually  avoid in part because he says actually  this is letter 6 rather he says when you  notice that your solitude is vast you  should be happy for what would a  solitude be that was not vast there is  only one solitude and it is vast heavy  difficult to bear now what he's  suggesting there is that we're all  sharing to some degree not just within  this condition that makes us each one of  us isolated and alone by ourselves but  that we're all in some respect together  in this aloneness as well that the  solitude that  I can explore and retreat into or avoid  and try to escape from in me is in some  respect the same solitude that you are  in or that Rainer Maria Rilke nor is  interlocutor is in so he has a very  strong you could say metaphysical  conception of this is being part of the  human condition he also talks about it  in relation to childhood at several  points that's not to say that solitude  only stems from or develops within  childhood but childhood is one period  where we can go back to and remember and  realize that we encountered it and that  we lived within it and if you if you  think about your own childhood however  you know great or traumatic it was there  you're going to find something similar  to what he's talking about he suggests  in his very first letter that his  interlocutor this guy coppice should  withdraw into his his own solitude he  says try to raise up the sunken feelings  of this enormous past your childhood  your personality will grow stronger your  solitude will expand and become a place  where you can live in the Twilight where  the noise of other people passes by far  in the distance and that is you know  coming about because of this this  movement back into childhood he also  talks about a child's attitude towards  adults and he says to be solitary this  is in letter six as you were when you  were a child when the grown-ups walked  around involved with matters that seemed  large and important because they looked  so busy and you didn't understand a  thing of what they were doing when you  realize their activities are shabby  their vocations are petrified and no  longer connected with life why not  continue to look on it as a child would  as if you were looking at something  unfamiliar out of the depths of your own  world from the vastness of your own  solitude and so within childhood  we already have and encounter with with  this now that doesn't mean that we fully  cognizant or understand its value or  even develop our solitude entirely it  depends on our stages of life and  adulthood is going to be important but  childhood is something we can go back to  it solitude should also be understood in  terms of growth and distance and there's  a really a two-fold aspect to this  Rilke talks about solitude itself as  something like interior space that it  can be smaller or it can be larger or  vast or wider he also talks about the  solitude of external spaces for example  when he is writing to Kappas from you  know Russia for example when he's  talking to campus about campus as a  military officer about where he's  stationed out and as we would say the  boonies or the sticks in the midst of  nature particularly when it's you know  wintertime and the snow is falling and  there's nobody else around there's this  this vast external landscape and we can  have something like that within  ourselves  this vast internal place so we can you  talked about our ability to grow  solitude and he suggests as I'm going to  talk about in a moment that we might do  that when things are in fact most  difficult for us when the solitude  itself is hard to bear that's when we  have the opportunity to grow that  solitude but I mentioned this is a  two-fold thing about growth and distance  it's not as if we're just sort of  opening up this space within ourselves  within whatever you want to conceive of  it our heart or mind our narrative we  are ourself growing within that solitude  as well so if we have too small of a  space of solitude not a lot of room for  us to grow we need to have  we need to develop enough space that we  can move around within it and there by  ourselves grow and deepen and even  connect ourselves back out to things you  might say outside of the bounds of our  own little solitude to that of the rest  of the world and to others he stresses  that solitude itself is very difficult  to bear and and it's quite  understandable we don't you know if you  want to translate solitude I'm Tom kite  as loneliness there is a lonely aspect  to it so he cautions campus he says in  the midst of the holiday you're bearing  your solitude more heavily than usual  more with more difficulty right he says  when you notice it is vast you should be  happy and this is where we have this  previous quote what would a solitude be  that was not vast and he says it is vast  heavy difficult to bear and almost  everyone has hours where he would gladly  exchange that solitude for any kind of  sociability however trivial or cheap for  the tiniest outward agreement with the  first person who comes along the most  unworthy but perhaps these are the very  hours during which solitude grows its  growing is as painful he says as the  growing of boys and as sad as the  beginning of spring but this must not  confuse you what is necessary after all  is only this solitude vast inner  solitude to walk inside yourself and  meet no one for hours that is what you  must be able to attain and that's  something that can be quite difficult  for many of us at you know many points  in our life he also writes a little bit  later this is in letter seven don't let  yourself be confused in your solitude by  the fact that there is something in you  that wants to move out of it he suggests  that if you use this desire prudently  you will in fact be able to expand that  solitude as well but there's this this  drive to get away from solitude  to lose ourselves in something where we  can have some easy agreement some some  superficiality some convention with  another person he also talks about you  know in some very important ways the  relationship between solitude and love  and I think this is a particularly  important thing to keep in mind because  you might think about solitude as  something that you have just by yourself  you could say well you know there is  this common human condition of solitude  but we're all these isolated monads you  know or individuals and atomized he's a  phrase that that sociologists  particularly like and none of us have  anything really in common none of us can  see into each other we're all sort of on  our own you come into this world alone  you die alone you live alone right well  that's not that's not the way Rilke  views solitude when solitude is used  correctly in order for us to grow when  we expand it when we are patient with it  it creates the possibility for us to  open up to another person and also to  see them as a person who has their own  solitude in which they have to grow we  don't have to be on top of them all the  time we don't have to be identical with  them we don't have to share everything  with them it's okay if they go off and  read their book or have their hobby and  do their thing over here and don't tell  you about it until they choose to  because that's what it means to have  actual human beings living with each  other it's not sharing everything all  the time but it's not being totally  closed off with each other either and in  letter 7 this is where he talks about  this the most and he says some very  important things here he tells us that  that you know solitude is difficult and  he tells us that love itself ties in  with solitude he says that learning time  is always a long secluded time  therefore loving for a long time ahead  and far out into life is what solitude a  heightened and deep and kind of  aloneness for the person who loves when  you love somebody else you really love  them you are in some respect on your own  loving doesn't mean pushing a button and  getting it the same response from them  and he goes on and he says that you know  it's it's a high inducement for the  individual to ripen to become something  in himself to become world to become  notice what he says here world hidden  himself for the sake of another person  when you love somebody else  you become if you do this more so that  you have more to offer them and you have  to do that by developing this solitude  within yourself you know we often to  have this trivial thing you have to love  yourself before you can love anybody  else this would be really loving  yourself developing what is good for you  this solitude so that you actually have  that to share with another person so  that when they withdraw and and you know  need some time to come back you're not  chasing them all the time and pressuring  them so that you're not saying dumb  stuff just to fill up the air with a lot  of nonsense so that you know there's  there's no quiet spaces or anything like  that you have to grow in order to be  capable of love and he talks about  temptations particularly for those who  he says are young but this would apply  to many older people who never develop  that solitude either who live lives that  are in some respects you know the the  proverbial lives of quiet desperation  right they can't be on their own so they  can't really be with another person  because they think that they're gonna  fuse with them and everything is going  to go great from that point on and Rilke  talks about them as throwing away their  solitude in doing that which is a big  problem comes back damaged unusable he  also has this very wonderful phrase  has been often discussed where he says  that what love would in fact really be  is in the highest degree is this two  solitudes protects and border and greet  each other and I think that it's  important he doesn't just say two  solitudes protect each other two  solitudes you know greet each other two  solitudes border each other  those three things those three elements  all need to be there because each of  them serves an important purpose they  protect each other they take care of  each other including protecting them the  other from themselves from imposing too  much or demanding too much of the other  they border the other they don't fuse  with the other they don't become one  single being they are they are two  different people but they border each  other in a way that other people don't  and they greet each other they have  something to say they have a positive  relation towards each other  Rilke is talking about romantic love in  this case but I don't see any reason why  that couldn't apply just as much to say  friendship or other types of  relationships perhaps inter familial  relationships as well the last thing  that I want to stress here is that Rilke  also views the solitary person as a sort  of path maker they are able to do things  that he thinks other human beings will  eventually be capable of if if humanity  does continue to develop but for the  moment this is something that that is  going to be restricted to the solitary  person he says he's talking about bodily  delight for example and he says people  have kind of screwed this up even with  eating and then he says the individual  can make these deep simple needs of life  clear for himself and live them clearly  not the individual who is dependent not  individuals taking their cues from say  the culinary industry or you know the  food channel or you know popular trends  about food or anything like that but the  person who's actually using their  solitude as a basis for having their own  experiences and thinking about things he  says not the individual who is dependent  but the solitary person the solitary  person has a scope of freedom that other  people do not and they can do things  that other people can't at this time a  little bit later he says everything that  may someday be possible for many people  the solitary person can now already  prepare and build with their own hands  which make fewer mistakes therefore love  your solitude and try to sing out with  the pain that it causes you because that  solitude provides you with this this  this freedom that other people don't yet  enjoy a freedom which allows you to  grasp things and take on tasks that  they're not up to so solitude runs  throughout all of these letters it's  absolutely central to Rilke as vision of  what it means to be a human being what  it means to develop and what it means to  live the good life particularly as an  artist  you
 

